          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Is there a case where two conditions documented together must NOT be linked by the coder?",
          "answer": "**Yes.** The main case is when the record says two conditions are unrelated. The \"with\" presumption does not apply then.\n\n- **The \"with\" presumption has limits.** Where the index links two conditions with \"with,\" the classification reads it as \"associated with\" or \"due to\" and presumes a link without the record stating one. But the presumption holds only for the pairings the index actually lists, and it is overridden when the documentation says the two are unrelated. (*ICD-10-CM code structure and the conventions that govern selection*; *Coding type 2 diabetes: E11*)\n\n- **Symptoms are not automatically linked to diagnoses.** A symptom code is used only when no more specific diagnosis is established. Once a diagnosis is documented, its code replaces the symptom code, with one exception: a symptom not routinely associated with that diagnosis is reported in addition, because it adds information the diagnosis does not. (*ICD-10-CM Chapter 18: symptoms, signs and abnormal findings*)\n\n- **One condition does not imply another.** Where asthma and chronic obstructive pulmonary disease are both documented, they are reported together. But an asthma code alone does not imply COPD, and the reverse is not true either. (*Coding asthma: J45*)\n\n- **Some conditions are classified elsewhere.** Sexually transmitted infections are coded in the infectious-disease chapter even when the site is genitourinary. Conditions complicating pregnancy belong to the obstetric chapter, which takes precedence. (*ICD-10-CM Chapter 14: genitourinary*)\n\nThe retrieved material does not include a full list of documented pairs that must be kept separate. The examples above are the ones it describes. For a complete set, the official ICD-10-CM Alphabetic Index and Tabular List conventions, or a certified coder, are the right sources. These passages are educational summaries.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
